1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,337
Today you build a real tool, and prove that it works.

2
00:00:03,887 --> 00:00:05,868
A tool that checks a data file,

3
00:00:05,868 --> 00:00:07,784
reports every problem clearly,

4
00:00:07,784 --> 00:00:09,701
and exits with the right code.

5
00:00:09,701 --> 00:00:11,426
Plus tests that catch bugs.

6
00:00:11,426 --> 00:00:15,642
This lesson puts the whole Python for DevOps masterclass together.

7
00:00:16,192 --> 00:00:19,477
Every demo is typed live on Ubuntu twenty-four oh four.

8
00:00:21,527 --> 00:00:23,561
A good utility has three parts.

9
00:00:23,561 --> 00:00:25,989
A pure function that checks the data,

10
00:00:25,989 --> 00:00:27,892
and returns a list of errors.

11
00:00:27,892 --> 00:00:32,092
A thin main function that reads the file and sets the exit code.

12
00:00:32,092 --> 00:00:32,748
And tests.

13
00:00:33,298 --> 00:00:36,150
Keeping the checking logic in a pure function,

14
00:00:36,150 --> 00:00:38,010
with no files and no printing,

15
00:00:38,010 --> 00:00:39,374
makes it easy to test.

16
00:00:39,374 --> 00:00:41,234
The main function stays small.

17
00:00:41,784 --> 00:00:43,175
Report every problem,

18
00:00:43,175 --> 00:00:44,698
not just the first one,

19
00:00:44,698 --> 00:00:46,353
and say which item it is.

20
00:00:46,353 --> 00:00:48,538
Exit zero when the data is valid,

21
00:00:48,538 --> 00:00:50,193
one when it has problems,

22
00:00:50,193 --> 00:00:52,643
and two when the file cannot be read.

23
00:00:53,193 --> 00:00:59,081
Test the edges: the largest valid value, and the first invalid one. Bugs love boundaries.

24
00:00:59,631 --> 00:01:03,748
Then prove the tests work: break the code on purpose, and watch a test fail.

25
00:01:05,798 --> 00:01:08,570
Our example checks a list of services,

26
00:01:08,570 --> 00:01:09,810
each with a name,

27
00:01:09,810 --> 00:01:11,414
a port and a protocol.

28
00:01:11,414 --> 00:01:11,852
First,

29
00:01:11,852 --> 00:01:13,238
the checking logic.

30
00:01:13,238 --> 00:01:14,040
Type: head,

31
00:01:14,040 --> 00:01:14,988
dash sixteen,

32
00:01:14,988 --> 00:01:16,593
service check dot P Y.

33
00:01:17,443 --> 00:01:23,629
The validate function loops over the items and collects errors in a list: a missing name,

34
00:01:23,629 --> 00:01:24,810
a duplicate name,

35
00:01:24,810 --> 00:01:29,467
a port outside one to sixty-five thousand five hundred thirty-five,

36
00:01:29,467 --> 00:01:32,386
and a protocol that is not T C P or U D P.

37
00:01:32,386 --> 00:01:33,776
It returns the list.

38
00:01:33,776 --> 00:01:34,680
Now the data.

39
00:01:34,680 --> 00:01:35,375
Type: cat,

40
00:01:35,375 --> 00:01:36,835
services dot jay son.

41
00:01:37,385 --> 00:01:38,480
Four services,

42
00:01:38,480 --> 00:01:40,670
with three planted problems.

43
00:01:40,670 --> 00:01:42,078
Web appears twice,

44
00:01:42,078 --> 00:01:44,581
D N S has port seventy thousand,

45
00:01:44,581 --> 00:01:47,710
and the API has H T T P as its protocol.

46
00:01:47,710 --> 00:01:48,727
Run the tool.

47
00:01:48,727 --> 00:01:49,978
Type: dot slash,

48
00:01:49,978 --> 00:01:51,699
service check dot P Y,

49
00:01:51,699 --> 00:01:53,342
services dot jay son.

50
00:01:54,392 --> 00:01:55,404
Three problems,

51
00:01:55,404 --> 00:01:56,551
found in one run,

52
00:01:56,551 --> 00:01:58,845
each with the name of the service,

53
00:01:58,845 --> 00:02:00,128
and a summary line.

54
00:02:00,128 --> 00:02:01,342
Now the exit code.

55
00:02:01,342 --> 00:02:02,084
Type: echo,

56
00:02:02,084 --> 00:02:02,557
dollar,

57
00:02:02,557 --> 00:02:03,501
question mark.

58
00:02:04,051 --> 00:02:05,601
One: problems found.

59
00:02:05,601 --> 00:02:08,003
Now a file that does not exist.

60
00:02:08,003 --> 00:02:09,243
Type: dot slash,

61
00:02:09,243 --> 00:02:10,947
service check dot P Y,

62
00:02:10,947 --> 00:02:12,265
nope dot jay son.

63
00:02:13,315 --> 00:02:18,883
A clear message, and no crash. Check the exit code. Type: echo, dollar, question mark.

64
00:02:19,433 --> 00:02:21,400
Two: the file could not be read.

65
00:02:21,400 --> 00:02:21,707
Zero,

66
00:02:21,707 --> 00:02:24,289
one and two each mean something different,

67
00:02:24,289 --> 00:02:26,195
and a pipeline can act on that.

68
00:02:28,245 --> 00:02:29,216
Now the tests,

69
00:02:29,216 --> 00:02:31,573
and the main idea: prove it works.

70
00:02:31,573 --> 00:02:32,890
Show the test file.

71
00:02:32,890 --> 00:02:33,584
Type: cat,

72
00:02:33,584 --> 00:02:35,456
test service check dot P Y.

73
00:02:36,006 --> 00:02:37,359
Four kinds of test.

74
00:02:37,359 --> 00:02:38,286
A valid list,

75
00:02:38,286 --> 00:02:39,924
including the top edge,

76
00:02:39,924 --> 00:02:43,487
port sixty-five thousand five hundred thirty-five.

77
00:02:43,487 --> 00:02:44,200
Bad ports,

78
00:02:44,200 --> 00:02:45,838
tried with parametrize.

79
00:02:45,838 --> 00:02:47,050
A duplicate name.

80
00:02:47,050 --> 00:02:48,403
And a missing name.

81
00:02:48,403 --> 00:02:49,045
Run them.

82
00:02:49,045 --> 00:02:49,971
Type: pytest,

83
00:02:49,971 --> 00:02:50,470
dash Q.

84
00:02:51,520 --> 00:02:52,485
Seven passed.

85
00:02:52,485 --> 00:02:57,091
Now break the tool on purpose: move the upper limit up by one.

86
00:02:57,091 --> 00:02:57,834
Type: sed,

87
00:02:57,834 --> 00:02:58,354
dash I,

88
00:02:58,354 --> 00:02:58,799
quote,

89
00:02:58,799 --> 00:02:58,948
S,

90
00:02:58,948 --> 00:02:59,394
slash,

91
00:02:59,394 --> 00:03:01,251
six five five three five,

92
00:03:01,251 --> 00:03:01,697
slash,

93
00:03:01,697 --> 00:03:03,479
six five five three six,

94
00:03:03,479 --> 00:03:03,925
slash,

95
00:03:03,925 --> 00:03:04,371
quote,

96
00:03:04,371 --> 00:03:06,005
service check dot P Y.

97
00:03:06,555 --> 00:03:07,175
Changed.

98
00:03:07,175 --> 00:03:08,725
Run the tests again,

99
00:03:08,725 --> 00:03:10,121
with no traceback.

100
00:03:10,121 --> 00:03:11,128
Type: pytest,

101
00:03:11,128 --> 00:03:11,671
dash Q,

102
00:03:11,671 --> 00:03:12,756
dash dash T B,

103
00:03:12,756 --> 00:03:13,298
equals,

104
00:03:13,298 --> 00:03:13,531
no.

105
00:03:14,581 --> 00:03:16,652
One failure: the bad port test,

106
00:03:16,652 --> 00:03:20,595
with the value sixty-five thousand five hundred thirty-six.

107
00:03:20,595 --> 00:03:22,533
The edge test caught the bug,

108
00:03:22,533 --> 00:03:25,473
and told us exactly which value got through.

109
00:03:25,473 --> 00:03:26,275
Put it back.

110
00:03:26,275 --> 00:03:26,943
Type: sed,

111
00:03:26,943 --> 00:03:27,411
dash I,

112
00:03:27,411 --> 00:03:27,811
quote,

113
00:03:27,811 --> 00:03:27,945
S,

114
00:03:27,945 --> 00:03:28,346
slash,

115
00:03:28,346 --> 00:03:29,950
six five five three six,

116
00:03:29,950 --> 00:03:30,351
slash,

117
00:03:30,351 --> 00:03:32,021
six five five three five,

118
00:03:32,021 --> 00:03:32,422
slash,

119
00:03:32,422 --> 00:03:32,823
quote,

120
00:03:32,823 --> 00:03:34,293
service check dot P Y.

121
00:03:34,843 --> 00:03:38,768
Fixed. Run all the tests once more. Type: pytest, dash Q.

122
00:03:39,818 --> 00:03:40,582
Seven passed.

123
00:03:40,582 --> 00:03:43,873
That is what a test suite is for: when the code changes,

124
00:03:43,873 --> 00:03:46,282
one run tells you whether it still works.

125
00:03:48,332 --> 00:03:49,417
Here is your task,

126
00:03:49,417 --> 00:03:50,925
with everything you need.

127
00:03:50,925 --> 00:03:52,854
The files are in the repository,

128
00:03:52,854 --> 00:03:54,241
and in the description.

129
00:03:54,791 --> 00:04:00,466
The repository is git hub dot com, slash it dash waves, slash dev ops dash A I.

130
00:04:01,016 --> 00:04:05,620
In the academy slash lesson fifty-five folder you will find the task file,

131
00:04:05,620 --> 00:04:06,367
the checker,

132
00:04:06,367 --> 00:04:07,923
check fifty-five dot S H,

133
00:04:07,923 --> 00:04:10,038
and a deliberately broken version,

134
00:04:10,038 --> 00:04:11,469
which the checker uses.

135
00:04:12,019 --> 00:04:13,464
You write two files.

136
00:04:13,464 --> 00:04:13,897
First,

137
00:04:13,897 --> 00:04:15,630
inventory check dot P Y,

138
00:04:15,630 --> 00:04:19,458
a tool that validates a jay son inventory of servers.

139
00:04:19,458 --> 00:04:21,769
Each server needs a unique name,

140
00:04:21,769 --> 00:04:23,719
an environment that is dev,

141
00:04:23,719 --> 00:04:24,874
staging or prod,

142
00:04:24,874 --> 00:04:26,607
and a valid I P address.

143
00:04:26,607 --> 00:04:28,918
It prints one error per problem,

144
00:04:28,918 --> 00:04:30,724
and exits with code zero,

145
00:04:30,724 --> 00:04:31,012
one,

146
00:04:31,012 --> 00:04:31,518
or two.

147
00:04:32,068 --> 00:04:32,536
Second,

148
00:04:32,536 --> 00:04:34,474
test inventory check dot P Y,

149
00:04:34,474 --> 00:04:36,078
with at least six tests,

150
00:04:36,078 --> 00:04:37,281
using parametrize.

151
00:04:37,281 --> 00:04:39,220
They must pass for your tool,

152
00:04:39,220 --> 00:04:41,358
and fail for the broken version,

153
00:04:41,358 --> 00:04:43,029
which accepts duplicates,

154
00:04:43,029 --> 00:04:44,767
accepts bad I P addresses,

155
00:04:44,767 --> 00:04:46,639
and ignores the environment.

156
00:04:47,189 --> 00:04:50,199
The checker needs no server and no cloud account.

157
00:04:50,199 --> 00:04:53,823
Do not open the solution files until you have really tried.

158
00:04:54,373 --> 00:04:58,043
The checker shows six pass lines. Write your results in a report.

159
00:05:00,093 --> 00:05:04,018
Here is how your work is judged. Six points, and you need all six.

160
00:05:04,568 --> 00:05:06,403
The checker shows six pass lines.

161
00:05:06,953 --> 00:05:11,689
The tool prints every problem on its own line, and exits with zero, one, or two.

162
00:05:12,239 --> 00:05:16,399
Your validate function follows every rule, with one error per problem.

163
00:05:16,949 --> 00:05:20,618
You have at least six tests, all passing, and you use parametrize.

164
00:05:21,168 --> 00:05:23,408
Your tests fail against the broken version.

165
00:05:23,958 --> 00:05:27,372
The report is complete, and you worked in your own virtual machine.

166
00:05:27,922 --> 00:05:29,543
To pass, you need all six.

167
00:05:31,593 --> 00:05:34,430
Your turn, in your own Ubuntu virtual machine.

168
00:05:34,980 --> 00:05:40,228
One: get the lesson folder, activate your virtual environment, and install pytest.

169
00:05:40,778 --> 00:05:45,834
Two: write the validate function first, and try it on small lists in the Python prompt.

170
00:05:46,384 --> 00:05:50,416
Three: add the command line part, and check the exit codes with echo.

171
00:05:50,966 --> 00:05:53,526
Four: write your tests, and include the edges.

172
00:05:54,076 --> 00:05:56,764
Five: break your own code, and watch a test fail.

173
00:05:57,314 --> 00:05:59,789
Six: run the checker, and write your report.

174
00:06:00,339 --> 00:06:02,174
Pause the video, and begin now.

175
00:06:17,724 --> 00:06:18,599
Quick check.

176
00:06:19,149 --> 00:06:21,623
Why keep the checking logic in a pure function?

177
00:06:22,173 --> 00:06:24,819
What do exit codes zero, one and two mean here?

178
00:06:25,369 --> 00:06:26,905
Which values should you test?

179
00:06:27,455 --> 00:06:29,268
How do you prove a test can fail?

180
00:06:29,818 --> 00:06:36,602
Objective covered: MC06-O09, a tested inventory validation utility.

181
00:06:37,152 --> 00:06:39,221
Pause the video, and answer out loud.

182
00:06:49,771 --> 00:06:53,078
Next lesson: Python for DevOps mastery evidence.

183
00:06:53,628 --> 00:06:56,956
